Q1 – Building Text Under Load: String, StringBuilder or StringBuffer?


1. Introduction
A railway reservation system generates e-tickets by combining information such as PNR, passenger name, coach, berth, fare and journey details.

Java provides three ways to create and modify strings:
1. String: Immutable. Once created, its value cannot be changed. Every concatenation using + creates a new String object.
2. StringBuilder: Mutable. It modifies the same character buffer without creating a new String object for every append. It is not thread-safe.
3. StringBuffer: Mutable and synchronized. It is thread-safe, but synchronization can make it slower than StringBuilder.
2. Prediction Before Implementation
Approach	Expected behaviour	Reason
String (+)	Slow for many fields	Creates new String objects during concatenation.
StringBuilder	Fast	Appends data to a reusable mutable buffer.
StringBuffer	Slightly slower than StringBuilder	Synchronization adds overhead.

My prediction: For a small ticket, the difference will be negligible. For thousands of tickets containing many fields, StringBuilder should perform better than the other approaches.
3. Three Designed Workloads
I would test the following workloads:
Workload	Number of tickets	Fields per ticket	Purpose
Workload 1	1	6	Test a small ticket.
Workload 2	10,000	50	Test heavy ticket generation.
Workload 3	1,000	500	Test tickets containing many fields.

These workloads help determine whether the difference increases with the number of tickets and fields.
4. Java Implementation
The following program implements all three approaches and measures execution time and approximate memory usage.

public class RailwayTicketBenchmark {

    static volatile long result;

    interface Task {
        long run();
    }

    // Approach 1: Using String +
    static long usingString(int tickets, String[] fields) {
        long total = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < tickets; i++) {
            String ticket = "";

            for (String field : fields) {
                ticket = ticket + field + "|";
            }

            total += ticket.length();
        }
        return total;
    }

    // Approach 2: Using StringBuilder
    static long usingStringBuilder(int tickets, String[] fields) {
        long total = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < tickets; i++) {
            StringBuilder ticket = new StringBuilder();

            for (String field : fields) {
                ticket.append(field).append("|");
            }

            String output = ticket.toString();
            total += output.length();
        }
        return total;
    }

    // Approach 3: Using StringBuffer
    static long usingStringBuffer(int tickets, String[] fields) {
        long total = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < tickets; i++) {
            StringBuffer ticket = new StringBuffer();

            for (String field : fields) {
                ticket.append(field).append("|");
            }

            String output = ticket.toString();
            total += output.length();
        }
        return total;
    }

    // Measuring execution time and memory
    static void measure(String name, Task task) {

        // Warm-up
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            result = task.run();
        }

        long totalTime = 0;

        System.gc();

        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();

        long memoryBefore =
            runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {

            long start = System.nanoTime();

            result = task.run();

            long end = System.nanoTime();

            totalTime += (end - start);
        }

        long memoryAfter =
            runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();

        System.out.println(name);

        System.out.println("Average Time: "
            + (totalTime / 5) + " ns");

        System.out.println("Approximate Memory Change: "
            + (memoryAfter - memoryBefore) + " bytes");

        System.out.println();
    }

    // Testing different workloads
    static void testWorkload(int tickets, int fieldCount) {

        String[] fields = new String[fieldCount];

        for (int i = 0; i < fieldCount; i++) {
            fields[i] = "Field" + (i + 1);
        }

        System.out.println("\nTickets: " + tickets);
        System.out.println("Fields: " + fieldCount);

        measure("String (+)",
            () -> usingString(tickets, fields));

        measure("StringBuilder",
            () -> usingStringBuilder(tickets, fields));

        measure("StringBuffer",
            () -> usingStringBuffer(tickets, fields));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Workload 1: Small ticket
        testWorkload(1, 6);

        // Workload 2: Heavy ticket generation
        testWorkload(10000, 50);

        // Workload 3: Large number of fields
        testWorkload(1000, 500);
    }
}
How to execute:
1. Open VS Code.
2. Create a Java file named RailwayTicketBenchmark.java.
3. Copy the above program.
4. Run the program.
5. Record the execution time and approximate memory change for all three approaches.
Note: Memory readings are approximate because Java garbage collection and JVM memory management affect the results. For more reliable benchmarking, JMH (Java Microbenchmark Harness) can be used.
5. Expected Results and Observations
The following is the expected performance trend, not fabricated benchmark measurements. Your actual measurements should be recorded after running the program on your computer.
Workload	String (+)	StringBuilder	StringBuffer
1 ticket, 6 fields	Fast	Fast	Fast
10,000 tickets, 50 fields	Slowest expected	Fastest expected	Slightly slower than StringBuilder
1,000 tickets, 500 fields	Much slower expected	Fastest expected	Slightly slower than StringBuilder

Expected observations:
- String performs reasonably for small tickets because only a few concatenations are required.
- String becomes inefficient when the number of fields increases because every concatenation creates a new String.
- StringBuilder performs better because it modifies its internal buffer.
- StringBuffer has similar functionality to StringBuilder but synchronization introduces additional overhead.
Intermediate String Objects Created
For a ticket containing n fields:
Approach	Intermediate objects
String	Approximately O(n) new String results per ticket
StringBuilder	No new String for each append; one final String when toString() is called
StringBuffer	No new String for each append; one final String when toString() is called

The String approach also produces repeated temporary character data. The builder approaches reuse their internal buffers.

Small correction to the benchmark: To measure the creation of the final printable ticket, change the two builder approaches to use toString() before measuring the length:
String output = ticket.toString();
total += output.length();
Use this in both the StringBuilder and StringBuffer methods instead of total += ticket.length();.
6. Comparison of Prediction and Observed Behaviour
My prediction is that StringBuilder will perform better for large workloads, while the difference between the three approaches will be small for a short ticket.

After running the program, the measured execution times can be compared with this prediction.

If the results show that StringBuilder is faster for large workloads, the prediction is supported. If the differences are small, JVM optimizations, hardware and garbage collection may have influenced the measurements.
7. Design Decision – Multithreaded Booking Server
The railway reservation system has two different requirements.

A. Per-ticket text – StringBuilder

I recommend StringBuilder because:
- Each thread can create its own ticket independently.
- It is faster than StringBuffer because it does not use synchronization.
- It avoids unnecessary intermediate String objects.
- It is suitable for building large tickets efficiently.
B. Shared booking log – StringBuffer

I would consider StringBuffer for a shared booking log because:
- Multiple threads may append booking information.
- Its append methods are synchronized.
- It helps prevent simultaneous modifications from interfering with each other.
However, if an entire booking-log entry requires multiple append operations, those operations should be protected by one synchronized block or another locking mechanism. StringBuffer does not automatically make a sequence of multiple operations atomic.

Final recommendation:
Requirement	Recommended class
Individual e-ticket	StringBuilder
Shared booking log	StringBuffer

8. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Instead of StringBuilder, we can use String.join(), StringJoiner or String.format().

Alternative 1: String.join()
String ticket = String.join("|", fields);
String.join() combines all elements of an array using the specified delimiter.

Alternative 2: StringJoiner
import java.util.StringJoiner;

StringJoiner ticket = new StringJoiner("|");

for (String field : fields) {
    ticket.add(field);
}

String output = ticket.toString();
StringJoiner provides a convenient way to join multiple strings with a delimiter.

Alternative 3: String.format()
String ticket = String.format(
    "PNR: %s | Name: %s | Coach: %s",
    "123456", "Arun", "S2"
);
String.format() is useful when the output requires a fixed format, but it is generally less suitable for repeatedly building large tickets because of formatting overhead.
Time Complexity Analysis
Let n represent the number of fields and assume each field has approximately equal length.
Approach	Time complexity	Explanation
String (+)	O(n²)	Repeatedly copies the growing string.
StringBuilder	O(n)	Appends fields to a mutable buffer.
StringBuffer	O(n)	Appends fields with synchronization overhead.
String.join()	O(n)	Processes and combines the fields.
StringJoiner	O(n)	Adds elements to a mutable internal structure.
String.format()	Depends on formatting	Formatting and conversion introduce additional overhead.

Strictly speaking, if the total number of characters across all fields is L, StringBuilder and String.join() take O(L) time, while repeated concatenation can take O(nL) in the equal-sized-field case.
Space Complexity Analysis
- String: Creates multiple intermediate String objects, resulting in O(n²) cumulative character allocation for equal-sized fields.
- StringBuilder: Uses an internal buffer and creates one final String, requiring O(L) space.
- StringBuffer: Uses an internal mutable buffer and creates one final String, requiring O(L) space.
- String.join(): Requires O(L) space for the resulting String.
- StringJoiner: Requires O(L) space for its internal contents and final result.
9. Conclusion
From this investigation, I conclude that the choice of String, StringBuilder and StringBuffer matters significantly when generating a large number of railway tickets.

String is suitable for simple and small text operations. StringBuilder is the best choice for generating individual e-tickets because it offers better performance and efficient memory usage. StringBuffer is useful when multiple threads need synchronized access to a shared string buffer.

Therefore, I recommend using StringBuilder for individual e-tickets and StringBuffer with appropriate synchronization for shared booking logs.
